<!-- RoboJuDo sim2sim scene for Table IX's terrain-traversal test (mujoco_locomotion_metrics_
worker.py). Identical to scene_g1_29dof.xml (same include, same robot, same keyframe) except the
flat "floor" plane is replaced by an <hfield> geom, still named "floor" so g1_29dof.xml's existing
foot<->floor contact pairs (friction=0.8, solref=0.01) keep applying to it unchanged -- see that
file's own comment on why the name matters.

HFIELD CONVENTION (empirically confirmed against a live MuJoCo instance before this file was
written, not assumed from docs -- world_z = hfield_data_normalized[0,1] * elevation_z, local to the
geom's own body frame): size="4 4 0.08 0.1" means an 8m x 8m physical patch (radius_x=radius_y=4.0,
matching training's own terrain_length=terrain_width=8.0 -- config_values/terrain.py), elevation_z
=0.08m (covers this project's own light_rough/rough/low_obstacles tiers' worst-case deviation,
-0.075m at difficulty 0.9 -- see mujoco_locomotion_metrics_worker.py's own _run_terrain_traversal
docstring for the exact per-tier formulas this is sized against), base_z=0.1m solid foundation
below the lowest point.

pos="0 0 -0.08" on the geom shifts the hfield's own local z=0 (hfield_data value 0.0) down to
world z=-0.08, so local z=elevation_z (hfield_data value 1.0) lands at world z=0 -- i.e. a
FLAT tile (hfield_data == 1.0 everywhere, this project's own "flat"/"low_obstacles" baseline)
reproduces the EXACT z=0 ground reference scene_g1_29dof.xml's own default_stand keyframe assumes,
so the loco-only/with-library velocity-axis and push-recovery tests (which reuse this same scene,
setting hfield_data to all-1.0 for those) see byte-identical ground behavior to the plain flat
scene. nrow=ncol=80 matches training's own horizontal_scale=0.1m at an 8m tile (8.0/0.1=80).

hfield_data itself is NOT set here -- it is written at runtime, per episode, via MjModel.
hfield_data (confirmed writable with no recompile needed) by _run_terrain_traversal, which reuses
this project's own real terrain-generation formulas (simulator/shared/terrain.py's
_rough_terrain_func/_low_obstacles_terrain_func/_flat_terrain_func, reproduced inline there since
that module's own trimesh import is not available in the robojudo conda env this worker runs
under -- see that function's own docstring for the exact citation). Defaults to all-zero (a flat
hfield, all data at the array's own zero-init) until first written, harmless since every episode
writes it before stepping. -->
<mujoco model="g1_29dof_holosoma_scene_terrain">
  <include file="g1_29dof.xml"/>

  <statistic center="1.0 0.7 1.0" extent="0.8"/>

  <visual>
    <headlight diffuse="0.6 0.6 0.6" ambient="0.1 0.1 0.1" specular="0.9 0.9 0.9"/>
    <rgba haze="0.15 0.25 0.35 1"/>
    <global azimuth="-140" elevation="-20"/>
  </visual>

  <asset>
    <texture type="skybox" builtin="flat" rgb1="0 0 0" rgb2="0 0 0" width="512" height="3072"/>
    <hfield name="terrain" nrow="80" ncol="80" size="4 4 0.08 0.1"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <light pos="1 0 3.5" dir="0 0 -1" directional="true"/>
    <geom name="floor" type="hfield" hfield="terrain" pos="0 0 -0.08" rgba="0.25 0.3 0.35 1"/>
  </worldbody>

  <!-- Identical to scene_g1_29dof.xml's own keyframe -- see that file's own comment. -->
  <keyframe>
    <key name="default_stand"
         qpos="0 0 0.756464 1 0 0 0
               -0.312 0 0 0.669 -0.363 0
               -0.312 0 0 0.669 -0.363 0
               0 0 0
               0.2 0.2 0 0.6 0 0 0
               0.2 -0.2 0 0.6 0 0 0"/>
  </keyframe>
</mujoco>
